---
// The whole argument in three numbers, placed early.
//
// This is not a restatement of the takeaways above it. The takeaways carry the
// findings; this carries the METHOD — the three things a reader needs to price
// their own redemption and then close the tab, if that is all they came for.
// Everything after it in the article is depth on these three lines.
//
// Deliberately not a component with inputs. A reader who wants to compute
// something has the tool further down; this one only has to be readable.
---

<aside class="sv" aria-label="The short version">
  <div class="sv-h">If you read nothing else</div>

  <ol class="sv-list">
    <li>
      <b>One cent is what doing nothing pays.</b>{' '}
      Every KrisFlyer mile can be cashed out at 100 miles = S$1 through Kris+, KrisShop,
      Pelago or Miles+Cash. Treat it as a <em>ceiling</em> on the lazy option rather than a floor
      beneath you &mdash; where the shop marks the price up, you get less than the cent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The cabin decides the value, not the airline.</b>{' '}
      The award chart charges roughly <b>2.1&ndash;2.5&times;</b> for business and about
      <b>3&times;</b> for a Suite. Cash fares separate those cabins by far more. Point a mile
      where the two ladders diverge and it is worth multiples of what it is worth anywhere else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Your card has one number to beat.</b>{' '}
      <span class="sv-eq">cashback rate &divide; miles per dollar</span>
      is the value per mile you must actually achieve to have been right. At 1.7% cashback and a
      1.4 mpd card, that is <b>1.21 cents</b>. Business class clears it comfortably. A cheap
      economy seat does not.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p class="sv-f">And one honest test before any of it: <b>would you have paid cash for that
  seat?</b> If the answer is no, the miles saved you the cheaper fare you would really have
  bought &mdash; not the expensive one you enjoyed.</p>
</aside>

